 "turn_2": {
  "type": "answer",
  "content": "**Conclusion**\nCHG-1 caused the incident. Do not roll back CHG-2.\n\n**Mechanism**\nCHG-1 (Tuesday) disabled memory tiering for the six SQL VMs and reserved their full 256 GB memory each. On esxi-pay-05, this consumed 256 GB of DRAM that was previously available for tiered VMs. Consequently, the \"DRAM left for tiered VMs\" shrank. The working set of the remaining 34 tiered VMs did not change, so their Active Memory as a percentage of the *remaining* DRAM jumped from ~35% to 77% (above the 75% threshold for significant loss). This forced sustained NVMe fetches (380 MB/s at 290 µs) starting Friday, not Tuesday, because the weekend peak load pushed the working set over the edge.\n\nCHG-2 (Thursday) added 40 small VMs (32 GB each). These VMs are tiered and fit within the NVMe tier. They did not consume the full DRAM reservations that CHG-1 did. Their contribution to Active Memory is minor compared to the SQL VMs' 256 GB full reservation.\n\n**What to check next**\n1. **Confirm CHG-1 impact:** Verify that esxi-pay-05’s DRAM capacity for tiered VMs dropped by exactly the sum of the SQL VM reservations on that host.\n2. **Immediate mitigation:** Migrate pay-api-07 (and other affected tiered VMs) to hosts with lower Active Memory % (e.g., esxi-pay-07 at 44%) to escape the NVMe latency penalty.\n3. **Long-term fix:** Re-enable memory tiering for the SQL VMs on esxi-pay-05, or migrate them to hosts with more DRAM, or add DRAM. Keeping them with tiering off and full reservation on a tiered host is the configuration error.\n\nNot settled by the knowledge base: Why DRS did not proactively rebalance the VMs off esxi-pay-05 before the Friday spike.",
